ent.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Enzo Fern&aacute;ndez, Chelsea &rarr; Manchester City.</span><span>&nbsp;Three mutually exclusive reports in twenty-four hours (see the secondary feature above). </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Contact made; everything beyond that is contested.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Carlos Baleba, Brighton &rarr; Manchester United.</span><span>&nbsp;Paused. Baleba damaged ankle ligaments in pre-season and is expected to miss the start of the season; Brighton are &ldquo;hopeful it is not long-term.&rdquo; United have instead spent on </span><span style="font-weight:700">Andrey Santos</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Youri Tielemans</span><span>&nbsp;and are now prioritising a left-back. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">On ice, injury-driven.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Jo&atilde;o Palhinha, Bayern &rarr; Aston Villa.</span><span>&nbsp;Romano reported personal terms agreed on 12 August, with the player having told Bayern he wants to go. Bayern want around &euro;25m against the roughly &euro;51m they paid in 2024; a loan with an option remains possible. Villa&rsquo;s head coach publicly acknowledged the interest on 8 August. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Personal terms agreed.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Kaishu Sano, Mainz &rarr; RB Leipzig.</span><span>&nbsp;Leipzig&rsquo;s interest accelerated by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Christoph Baumgartner</span><span>&rsquo;s injury. Contract to 2028, no release clause, and a Mainz chief executive in no mood: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Christian Heidel</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;You wouldn&rsquo;t even get one of his legs for &euro;25m&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; also calling Sano &ldquo;the heart of our game.&rdquo; Liverpool are among others watching. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Concrete interest.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Rafael Le&atilde;o, AC Milan &rarr; Galatasaray.</span><span>&nbsp;A bid of &euro;30m plus &euro;5m was rejected on 6 August as inadequate, per </span><span style="font-weight:700">Di Marzio</span><span>; Galatasaray are preparing to go again. Le&atilde;o has told Milan he wants to stay and, on 11 August, that &ldquo;nobody other than my family and lawyers are authorised to speak for me.&rdquo; </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Bid rejected; player resisting.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Exequiel Palacios, Bayer Leverkusen &rarr; Ipswich Town.</span><span>&nbsp;A genuine Romano exclusive on 9 August &mdash; Ipswich lodged an official bid, with four other Premier League and Bundesliga clubs in the race. By 12 August the bid had been rejected: Leverkusen want &euro;35m and the World Cup winner is reported to want a bigger club. Ipswich have instead agreed a fee with </span><span style="font-weight:700">Strasbourg</span><span>&nbsp;for </span><span style="font-weight:700">Julio Enciso</span><span>. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Bid rejected.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Gabriel Jesus &rarr; Napoli.</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight:700">Sky Sport Italia</span><span>&nbsp;placed his agent at Napoli&rsquo;s training camp on 8 August, with the player said to have approved a move conditional on two Napoli departures. The agent publicly denied talks on 7 August. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Rumour, denied.</span><span>&nbsp;Napoli&rsquo;s defensive alternative, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Beno&icirc;t Badiashile</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;from Chelsea, has stalled over the formula &mdash; Napoli offered a &euro;3m loan with an option; Chelsea raised their price on 12 August.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Mika Baur, Paderborn &rarr; Celtic</span><span>&nbsp;(medical expected 12 August, package reported up to &euro;8m) and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fabio Bald&eacute;, HSV &rarr; Strasbourg</span><span>&nbsp;(&euro;7m plus bonuses, travelling to France on 12 August) are the two Bundesliga-adjacent deals most likely to be official by the time you read this. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Medical stage.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">And one to file away.</span><span>&nbsp;On 11 August </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fabrizio Romano</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; the single most-read transfer reporter alive &mdash; wrote on LinkedIn that he is &ldquo;probably not gonna continue into the transfer market industry as I&rsquo;m doing right now for longer: it&rsquo;s time to change the approach, the view.&rdquo; He said he would say more after the window closes on 3 September. The post followed a public argument on X over the reporting of the Diom&aacute;nde fee. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Single-sourced; his own words, via LinkedIn.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">AROUND THE LEAGUES</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">England &middot; Premier League</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The season opens on </span><span style="font-weight:700">Friday 21 August</span><span>&nbsp;and it will begin with the most comprehensively rebuilt set of dugouts in the competition&rsquo;s history. Twelve of the twenty clubs will start with a different head coach from the one who finished last season. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Pep Guardiola</span><span>&nbsp;has gone from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester City</span><span>&nbsp;and been replaced by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Enzo Maresca</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Andoni Iraola</span><span>&nbsp;is at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Liverpool</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Xabi Alonso</span><span>, sacked by Real Madrid in January, is at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Chelsea</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Michael Carrick</span><span>&nbsp;was made permanent head coach at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester United</span><span>&nbsp;on 22 May, replacing </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ruben Amorim</span><span>, who is now at Milan. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Oliver Glasner</span><span>&nbsp;has moved to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nottingham Forest</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Pierre Sage</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Crystal Palace</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Marco Rose</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bournemouth</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">&aacute;lvaro Arbeloa</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fulham</span><span>, and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Matthias Jaissle</span><span>&nbsp;to Newcastle. The three promoted clubs &mdash; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Coventry City</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Hull City</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ipswich Town</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; have brought </span><span style="font-weight:700">Frank Lampard</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sergej Jakirovi&#263;</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Gary O&rsquo;Neil</span><span>&nbsp;with them. Only </span><span style="font-weight:700">David Moyes</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Everton</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Unai Emery</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Aston Villa</span><span>&nbsp;feel like fixed points. For the standings context that shapes all of it: the 2025/26 title went to Arsenal on 85 points, and </span><span style="font-weight:700">West Ham</span><span>&nbsp;were relegated in eighteenth on 39, alongside </span><span style="font-weight:700">Burnley</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Wolves</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The pre-season evidence is thin and mostly unflattering. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Liverpool</span><span>&nbsp;lost 3&ndash;2 at home to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Monaco</span><span>&nbsp;on 9 August in front of 60,216 &mdash; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Alexander Isak</span><span>&nbsp;on 16 and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Florian Wirtz</span><span>&nbsp;on 29, then </span><span style="font-weight:700">Aleksandr Golovin</span><span>&nbsp;from the spot on 44, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Mika Biereth</span><span>&nbsp;on 56 and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Paris Brunner</span><span>&nbsp;on 88 &mdash; in Iraola&rsquo;s first game at Anfield. He made eleven changes at half-time and was unusually candid afterwards: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;We played a good first half but we don&rsquo;t have much more in terms of the level required to play like this,&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;adding that his players &ldquo;probably don&rsquo;t have the energy for 90 minutes.&rdquo; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester City</span><span>&nbsp;beat </span><span style="font-weight:700">Atl&eacute;tico Madrid</span><span>&nbsp;3&ndash;1 in Seoul the same day, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Omar Marmoush</span><span>&nbsp;scoring twice in two minutes with both goals made by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Antoine Semenyo</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;without him, these goals wouldn&rsquo;t have been possible; I just had to tap it in,&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;Marmoush said. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Chelsea</span><span>&nbsp;drew 3&ndash;3 with </span><span style="font-weight:700">Johor Darul Ta&rsquo;zim</span><span>&nbsp;in Malaysia, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Liam Delap</span><span>&nbsp;scoring two penalties. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester United</span><span>&nbsp;drew 1&ndash;1 with </span><span style="font-weight:700">PSG</span><span>&nbsp;in Gothenburg on 8 August, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bryan Mbeumo</span><span>&nbsp;cancelling out </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ibrahim Mbaye</span><span>; Carrick called it &ldquo;a good challenge&hellip; we should&rsquo;ve scored more goals.&rdquo; The season&rsquo;s first silverware is on Sunday: the </span><span style="font-weight:700">FA Community Shield</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">, unusually at the Principality Stadium in Cardiff rather than Wembley.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>Off the pitch, two structural items matter more than any signing. The first is that </span><span style="font-weight:700">PSR is gone</span><span>: clubs have approved a Squad Cost Ratio system to replace it, while voting down a proposal that would have introduced a salary cap. The second is that </span><span style="font-weight:700">Chelsea</span><span>&nbsp;are operating under a March 2026 settlement with the league &mdash; a &pound;10.75m fine and a </span><span style="font-weight:700">suspended one-year transfer ban</span><span>&nbsp;over Abramovich-era payments &mdash; which lends a certain edge to a summer in which they have spent &pound;117m on </span><span style="font-weight:700">Morgan Rogers</span><span>&nbsp;and this week added </span><span style="font-weight:700">Pep Chavarr&iacute;a</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Rayo Vallecano</span><span>&nbsp;for &euro;19m plus &euro;2m on a five-year deal. In squad news: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Micky van de Ven</span><span>&nbsp;signed a new long-term contract at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Tottenham</span><span>&nbsp;on 10&ndash;11 August (</span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve always loved Spurs, from the first day I stepped in here&rdquo;</span><span>); </span><span style="font-weight:700">Crystal Palace</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Everton</span><span>&nbsp;executed a straight player-for-player swap on 11 August, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Brennan Johnson</span><span>&nbsp;to Goodison and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Dwight McNeil</span><span>&nbsp;to Selhurst Park, both on four-year deals with no cash element disclosed; and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fulham</span><span>&nbsp;signed </span><span style="font-weight:700">Shea Charles</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Southampton</span><span>&nbsp;for an undisclosed fee widely reported at &pound;30m. The injury list is already ugly: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Amadou Onana</span><span>&nbsp;has ruptured a cruciate ligament at Aston Villa, and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Carlos Baleba</span><span>&nbsp;will miss the start of the season at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Brighton</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">.</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Spain &middot; LaLiga</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Spain is first of the big five out of the blocks &mdash; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Saturday 15 August</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Alav&eacute;s</span><span>&nbsp;against </span><span style="font-weight:700">Getafe</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; but only in a technical sense, because LaLiga has postponed four of the six opening fixtures to protect the workload of players who went deep into a World Cup that Spain won. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Atl&eacute;tico</span><span>&nbsp;v </span><span style="font-weight:700">M&aacute;laga</span><span>&nbsp;moves to 19 August; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Valencia</span><span>&nbsp;v </span><span style="font-weight:700">Real Betis</span><span>&nbsp;to the 25th; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Real Madrid</span><span>&nbsp;v </span><span style="font-weight:700">Real Sociedad</span><span>&nbsp;to the 26th; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Barcelona</span><span>&nbsp;v </span><span style="font-weight:700">Athletic Club</span><span style="colo